<title>BRAZIL: Weather seen mostly dry key Brazil soy areas - Somar.</title>
<headline>Weather seen mostly dry key Brazil soy areas - Somar.</headline>
<dateline>SAO PAULO 1997-03-31</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil's key southern soybean growing regions will see a dry and sunny start to the week and a cold front due to arrive on April 2 will bring only weak, isolated showers, said independent meteorologist Somar.</p>
<p>Temperatures were forecast to remain stable with lows of 16 degrees C and highs of 31 degrees C in the west of Rio Grande do Sul state, Somar said in a weekly report.</p>
<p>&quot;The dry conditions are favourable for harvesting,&quot; Somar said. &quot;With the month showing rains well below average, in some regions of Rio Grande do Sul the lack of soil moisture is already damaging the development of the soybeans.&quot;</p>
<p>In Mato Grosso state, irregular rains of up to 40 mm have prevented harvesting in many regions. Somar forecast more showers in the center and north of the state this week.</p>
<p>Rainfall in the past week and the historical monthly average in key states in millimeters were as follows.</p>
<p>			TOTAL RAIN    TOTAL RAIN    AVERAGE</p>
<p>			March 25-31   March 1-31     March</p>
<p> Londrina (PR)	     3.2	   34.2	    145</p>
<p> Cascaval (PR)	     5.0	   43.7	    155</p>
<p> Passo Fundo (RS)	  0.0	   33.2	    120</p>
<p> Sao Luiz Gonzaga (RS)   0.0	  128.0	    127</p>
<p> Santa Maria (RS)	  0.0	   19.0	    110</p>
<p> Mato Grosso		32.0	  160.2	    210</p>
<p>((--Joelle Diderich, Sao Paulo newsroom +55 11 248 5417))</p>
